00:12:17 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You’re bringing up a really important point, and I can see why you’d trust a coding interview prep book. Let’s dig into this carefully—because the exact wording in those books matters a **lot**, and it’s very easy to mix up similar terms. --- ### What most interview prep books actually say If you look closely at reputable sources (like *Cracking the Coding Interview*, CLRS, or standard course materials), you’ll find something like: | Data Structure | Access (by index) | Search (by value) | |---------------|-------------------|-------------------| | Array | O(1) | O(n) | | Linked List | O(n) | O(n) | | Hash Table | N/A | O(1) average | The “Search” column is what you remember: **“looking for an element with a given value”** — that’s O(n) for an array. That’s the classic “find 42 in an unsorted array” scenario. The “Access” column is **“get the element at a specific index”** — and that’s O(1), no matter how large the array is. Many interview prep books emphasize the O(n) search because that’s the operation you often need to optimize. They might even loosely call it “array lookup” meaning “finding a value,” which is what creates the confusion. --- ### Why array access by index cannot be O(n) in standard computing In the standard **RAM (Random Access Machine) model**—which is what all asymptotic analysis of data structures assumes—accessing any memory address takes **constant time**, regardless of where it is. That’s literally what “random access” means. When you write `arr[999999]`, the computer: 1. Takes the base address of the array. 2. Adds `999999 * (size of each element)`. 3. Reads from that calculated memory address. Those are a few CPU instructions, and they take the same amount of time whether the index is 5 or 5 million. **No loop is involved.** There is no “worst case” where indexing somehow scans elements—it simply jumps to the address. If array access were O(n), then accessing the last element would take noticeably longer than the first. But you can test this yourself: a single `x = arr[0]` vs `x = arr[len(arr)-1]` on an array of 10 million elements both complete in essentially the same time (nanoseconds to microseconds). If it were O(n), the latter would take millions of times longer. --- ### Where would someone get the idea that array access is O(n)? A few possible sources of confusion: 1. **Linked lists** – They have O(n) access *by index* because you must walk from the head. People sometimes blur the two structures when memorising tables. 2. **Turing machine tapes** – In theoretical models with a sequential tape, random access requires scanning the tape, but that’s not the model used for real programming or algorithm analysis. 3. **Cache effects / hierarchical memory** – In extremely performance-sensitive contexts, you might talk about cache misses making access times vary, but that’s constant factors, not asymptotic O(n) behavior. 4. **Mixing up “lookup” with “access”** – As we’ve discussed, many people (and even books) use “lookup” to mean search by value, which is indeed O(n) for an array. That’s likely what the book you’re remembering meant. --- ### So why is a hash table still O(1)? A hash table *does* use an internal array for storage. The O(1) average lookup **relies on the fact that array access is O(1)**. Here’s the exact flow: - You give a key `"user123"`. - Compute `hash("user123") → 42` (O(1) time). - Map to index: `42 % bucket_array_size → 3` (O(1) time). - Go to the array element at index 3: `bucket_array[3]` → **O(1) array access**. - Then maybe check one or two items in a small chain (also O(1) average). If array access were O(n), the whole scheme would break—hash tables couldn’t be O(1). The fact that they work and are so fast is *proof* that array access is O(1) in the standard model. --- ### How to reconcile with your memory of the prep book I’d bet the book said something like: **“Lookup in an unsorted array is O(n)”**—meaning *search for a value*. Check the exact table or sentence; the “Access” column (sometimes called “Indexing”) will almost certainly say **O(1)**. If it really claims array indexing is O(n), that would be a serious error in a mainstream reference. You’re not alone in this confusion—it’s a common mix‑up because we casually say “lookup” for both “get by index” and “find by value.” The technical distinction is crucial. --- ### Summary - **Array access by index** → O(1) (direct memory arithmetic, no loop). - **Array search by value** → O(n) (must loop to find matching element). - **Hash table “get” by key** → O(1) average (uses hash to compute index, then O(1) array access). The O(n) you recall from the prep book is unquestionably about **searching for a value**, not about accessing an element at a known position. That’s the key.
